turning now to one of the hardest  working filmmakers in the movie industry  we're talking about the great ava  duvernay  who is expressing her commitment to a  more inclusive hollywood with a brand  new initiative  it's a database called array crew and it  connects hiring managers and talented  crew members in film and tv  who are women and people of color here's  an example of why this is needed look at  the numbers an analysis  of 300 top grossing films found less  than one percent of camera operators  were female  and only six percent of editors were  people of color  their database is a partnership between  ava duvernay and former  warner brothers tv exec that's peter  roth duvernay  is also committed to inclusion in her  own projects  every single episode of her  groundbreaking series clean sugar  on own as a woman in the director's  chair go eva  here's one of the show stars bianca  lawson on why that's so important  queen sugar is really a trailblazer the  innovator of  having all female directors having a  totally  inclusive crew and cast and  such diversity that we rarely ever see  in other shows and  sets so i really feel like clean sugar  has really been at the forefront  of a shift that's happening right now  within the business  a beautiful shift i'll say ava ava  duvernay joins us now there she is the  brains and the beauty behind what bianca  calls this  beautiful shift so ava good morning to  you we're all here at the table so good  morning  friends good morning i know that you've  been working on this thinking about this  for at least the past two years  what is it that you saw that you said we  need to do something and by we you said  i'm gonna do something  well i would just walk on sets and not  see enough people who look not  like me not enough women not enough  people of color not enough people who  are  older even you know um um over 60  being older in our in our industry not  enough people with disabilities i mean  it's really the  idea that our our crews should reflect  the real world and they can  so often though when you talk to people  about why why don't we have more  inclusion on our sets they just say well  we don't know where to find people  so i thought well maybe we can help with  that so what was your pitch to the  studios number one how many people have  signed on  and what was your pitch you i cannot  even believe i'm saying this  every single major studio is signed on  wow  and streamer over the last two years  very detailed conversations  um have signed on and the pitch was  really  um you are making so much so many  shows and films right now why not get a  little help with crewing up  hey ava it's a big deal and a big  accomplishment just to have built the  database but i'm curious going forward  how will you monitor if at all whether  it's being implemented and used  as it should be well we have a whole  system of  checks and balances for people to really  report into us the hiring side producers  are you finding people on crew and then  crew members to have this kind of badge  of honor when they get  hired off of crew we have 70 productions  currently using it  across all of the studios there are 3  000 crew members in the database now  and it's growing every day yeah it  sounds like an industry story ava but do  you see it bigger than the industry  i do it might sound like it's just about  hollywood but really  we all consume we all watch and enjoy  what comes out of hollywood and so  it matters the way that it's made it  matters the intention behind these  things that there is  inclusion that there is belonging  because i believe that the way that the  set feels and looks  embeds itself in the image you end up  watching the way it was made so why  don't we  create a more balanced way of making the  work so that we can enjoy it even more  ava when you look on imdb and you look  up your name  it says you have 14 upcoming projects  how do you keep track of 14 upcoming  projects how do the pots on the stove  not all boil over  and anthony she doesn't look tired no  you know i i you know i i there there  are women before me who have juggled  oprah shonda uh men before me jj  spielberg these are the people i aspire  to and so i follow in their footsteps  let's talk about one of those projects  if you can just pick one out for now to  talk about let's talk about queen  sugar which uh shut down production  because of the pandemic then reopen and  as i understand it  you've reworked the storyline to bring  covet and black  black lives matter into the script what  was that process like  well we had written all of the episodes  and uh and then and then the pandemic  hit and so we went down like the rest of  hollywood when we came back we thought  well we can't use those old episodes  that don't address everything that  happened in 2020 so we decided to  rewrite it very quickly  and it is a show that really uh the  season is a time capsule of 2020  all the things that everyone went  through you know the fear about you know  covet and the election and and black  lives matter and  you know at food scarcity i think it's a  tribute to the survival  that we all have gone through and making  it through 2020. i thought this was cool  ava for the first time a poster of queen  sugar  was on the warner brothers lot listen i  was with queen sugar from the very  beginning i remember when you were  writing it  what was it like for you to see a poster  and warner brothers  it started as a small show and it really  does have a fervent cult following  yeah but the audience grows each season  and so for us to be  uh you know on the big the billboard on  the side of the studio was a real real  honor real pleasure a thrill  we've been talking a lot uh about cicely  tyson  uh and gail's last interview with her  and some of the words of wisdom that she  left all of us with  that continue to echo uh you you're  familiar with the work you knew her over  the years are there particular things  that she said or that you heard  her say uh that stick with you oh gosh  i'll get emotional and gail's last  interview with her was  extraordinary and she said everything  that she wanted to say  uh the last uh acting um that she did  was on  our show uh cherish today she was one of  the stars of our show  and so i got to know her really well she  was supportive of me since my very first  film  12 years ago the main thing she said to  me is don't worry  just keep working hard don't worry keep  your eye on the ball and just keep going  we get so bogged down and  in in the little pieces and the worry  and she said make sure you take keeping  your eye on the bigger picture  don't worry keep going and uh i hear you  say that to me and i intend to  she actually went to your first film at  the only  theater it was playing in on the east  coast when it came out  you went to the oh it was the only  theater in washington dc i get a call  out of the blue  i'm able to book this one small theater  somehow her friend mignon  took her to see this independent film by  someone that she didn't know  she called me after she said my dear  this is cecily tyson i just want to tell  you  your film is lovely and i want you to  keep going and we had a lovely  conversation  and we stayed in touch ever since what a  thrill for my path my little path to  cross with her  magnificent life so i miss her um but  well done right 96 years of beauty  ava thank you so much congratulations  congratulations  bravo the new season of queen sugar airs  on tuesdays on  own

